<p>Load and noise ratings vary by manufacturer and model. See <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for pricing factors.</p>
<h2 id="design-heading">Design Considerations</h2>
<p><strong>Structural support loading:</strong> Overhead conveyors transfer product weight plus conveyor weight to the building structure through hangers and support brackets. Calculate point loads at each hanger (product + trolley + chain + track weight) and verify building structural capacity. Existing buildings may require structural reinforcement. New construction should design overhead conveyor loads into the building steel from the start.</p>
<p><strong>Carrier and fixture design:</strong> The carrier (load bar, hook, fixture) connects the product to the trolley. Carrier design affects product accessibility during processing, loading/unloading ergonomics, and product security during transport. Custom carriers for automotive bodies, appliance shells, and aerospace parts represent significant engineering effort. Carriers must withstand paint chemicals, oven temperatures, and mechanical handling forces.</p>
<p><strong>Chain selection and tensioning:</strong> Overhead conveyor chains run under tension from the product load, friction, elevation changes, and curves. Chain pull calculation accounts for total suspended load, coefficient of friction, track inclines, and speed. Chain material and lubricant must suit the operating temperature: standard carbon steel for ambient zones, heat-treated alloy steel and high-temperature lubricant for ovens, stainless steel for corrosive environments. Get temperature ratings from the chain maker. Take-up mechanisms (screw, spring, or gravity) maintain proper chain tension.</p>
<p><strong>Integration with process equipment:</strong> Overhead conveyors must interface with spray booths (product positioning, booth openings, airflow management), ovens (temperature ratings, door openings, dwell time control), wash systems (spray coverage, drainage, chemical resistance), and loading/unloading stations (<a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">vertical lifts</a>, ergonomic work heights, automation interface).</p>
<p><strong>Safety systems:</strong> Overhead conveyors require emergency stops along the entire route, load-drop protection (catch pans, guards), pinch-point guarding at drive and take-up locations, and anti-backup devices on inclines. Power-and-free systems add zone-level stop controls and <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation zone</a> sensing for collision prevention. <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a> is the US conveyor safety standard covering design, installation, inspection, and operation, so ask suppliers to confirm compliance.</p>
