<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is an overhead conveyor system?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>An overhead conveyor uses elevated track to transport suspended products through production processes. Types include monorail (constant speed), power-and-free (independent trolley control), and enclosed track (clean, quiet). See our <a href="/conveyor-types/chain-conveyors/">chain conveyor hub</a> for the full chain conveyor family.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">Monorail vs power-and-free, which do I need?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Monorail for simple continuous flow at constant speed (single paint color, uniform cure time). Power-and-free for complex routing, variable dwell times, accumulation, and multi-path production. Power-and-free costs substantially more but handles mixed-model production and process variability.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What industries use overhead conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Automotive paint and assembly, powder coating, appliance finishing, garment handling, <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">meat processing</a>, aerospace manufacturing, and heavy equipment assembly. Any operation that benefits from floor-clear transport and overhead process routing.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How much weight can an overhead conveyor carry?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>It depends on system and track size. Ultimation rates its enclosed track trolley at 250 lbs and its 3-inch and 4-inch I-beam trolleys at 200 and 400 lbs (double with a load bar). Power-and-free unit loads run up to 20,000 lbs on heavy systems. Load capacity depends on track profile, trolley wheels, chain strength, and structural support. Heavier loads require larger I-beam track and reinforced building structure.</p>

</div>
</div>
